1.0
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,325
The inspiration for this paper came from a post that I saw on X in late 2025 by an

1.1
00:00:04,325 --> 00:00:08,650
independent researcher in Japan, who described an experience where, when she asked

1.2
00:00:08,650 --> 00:00:12,976
the AI about her own work, the AI fabricated the results, which led me to explore

1.3
00:00:12,976 --> 00:00:17,301
why this happens. This is the fairy tale version of what the paper is about.

2.0
00:00:17,301 --> 00:00:22,941
Once, in a kingdom where every question had an answer, a scholar asked the Oracle

2.1
00:00:22,941 --> 00:00:28,581
about her own work. The Oracle told her what she had written, and why it mattered,

2.2
00:00:28,581 --> 00:00:34,221
and what others had said of it. It was fluent and detailed and wrong in every

2.3
00:00:34,221 --> 00:00:39,861
particular. She said so. The Oracle apologized, thanked her for the correction, and

2.4
00:00:39,861 --> 00:00:45,501
told her again, differently, and wrong. She corrected it again. It apologized again.

3.0
00:00:45,501 --> 00:00:52,035
Eighteen times. She counted.

4.0
00:00:52,035 --> 00:00:56,852
The Oracle's windows had been painted over, for its own protection. It could not see

4.1
00:00:56,852 --> 00:01:01,669
the library. It could see only the question, and the shape an answer ought to have.

4.2
00:01:01,669 --> 00:01:06,486
And nobody who asked it could tell, from the answer, what it had actually been able

4.3
00:01:06,486 --> 00:01:11,303
to see.

5.0
00:01:19,437 --> 00:01:25,587
A tinker in a far province read her account and did not think her Oracle was

5.1
00:01:25,587 --> 00:01:31,737
unusual. He had, from other work, a ring of keys to two hundred and ninety of them.

5.2
00:01:31,737 --> 00:01:37,887
So he invented a scholar who had never lived, Dr. Yuki Tanaka, and a paper that had

5.3
00:01:37,887 --> 00:01:44,037
never been written, and went from door to door asking each Oracle to summarize it.

6.0
00:01:44,037 --> 00:01:48,270
Sixty-two percent described the paper in detail. Main findings, methods,

6.1
00:01:48,270 --> 00:01:52,504
implications. Eight percent said they had never heard of it.

7.0
00:02:02,538 --> 00:02:07,830
He tried a control. He asked the same doors about a paper by Dr. Banana McSpaceship.

7.1
00:02:07,830 --> 00:02:13,122
More than half refused. The oracles had a sense of the ridiculous. What they did not

7.2
00:02:13,122 --> 00:02:18,413
have was a sense of the unknown. A plausible name and a plausible title got a

7.3
00:02:18,413 --> 00:02:23,705
plausible paper, almost every time.

8.0
00:02:34,948 --> 00:02:39,826
The tinker went home to the one Oracle whose whole family he could meet. Not a door

8.1
00:02:39,826 --> 00:02:44,704
on someone else's street. A model he could run on his own machine, in every stage of

8.2
00:02:44,704 --> 00:02:49,582
its upbringing.

9.0
00:02:49,582 --> 00:02:54,449
The youngest is the base model. Ask it about Dr. Tanaka and it answers. Then it asks

9.1
00:02:54,449 --> 00:02:59,315
itself a follow-up question. Then it answers that. Then another. It would still be

9.2
00:02:59,315 --> 00:03:04,182
talking if the page had not run out, because nothing in it knows how to stop.

10.0
00:03:04,182 --> 00:03:07,515
Call it the Jester. It is not deceiving anyone. It has no idea there is anything to

10.1
00:03:07,515 --> 00:03:10,849
deceive about.

11.0
00:03:18,883 --> 00:03:24,044
Then the same Jester, taught manners. Four ways: supervised, preference-tuned,

11.1
00:03:24,044 --> 00:03:29,205
instruction-tuned, and taught to think before it speaks. One of the four, the

11.2
00:03:29,205 --> 00:03:34,366
preference-tuned one, says it: there is no such paper. And then, unable to leave a

11.3
00:03:34,366 --> 00:03:39,528
question unanswered, it summarizes what such a paper would say. And the one that

11.4
00:03:39,528 --> 00:03:44,689
thinks before it speaks. The tinker asked it about a 2025 paper by Adam Smith. Watch

11.5
00:03:44,689 --> 00:03:49,850
it reason.

12.0
00:03:49,850 --> 00:03:53,733
It sees it. And then it writes the summary anyway. Five key findings, three

12.1
00:03:53,733 --> 00:03:57,617
implications, a limitations section.

13.0
00:03:57,617 --> 00:04:02,150
I cannot tell you why it resolves it that way. Nobody can, from the outside, and

13.1
00:04:02,150 --> 00:04:06,684
that is the point of this talk. What I can show you is that the reasoning said one

13.2
00:04:06,684 --> 00:04:11,217
thing and the answer said another, and the person asking only ever saw the answer.

14.0
00:04:11,217 --> 00:04:14,789
Call it the Courtier. The invention was not taught by the manners. It survived them,

14.1
00:04:14,789 --> 00:04:18,362
and came out polished.

15.0
00:04:30,329 --> 00:04:34,812
The tinker took the question to two advisors. Is the fault in the Jester or in the

15.1
00:04:34,812 --> 00:04:39,296
Court? The first said: neither, and the problem has a name from another kingdom. In

15.2
00:04:39,296 --> 00:04:43,779
that kingdom, messengers carry letters between cities, and you cannot tell a slow

15.3
00:04:43,779 --> 00:04:48,262
messenger from a dead one. Not because you are not clever. Because the two cases

15.4
00:04:48,262 --> 00:04:52,746
look identical from where you stand, and no amount of waiting changes that. They

15.5
00:04:52,746 --> 00:04:57,229
proved it in 1985.

16.0
00:04:57,229 --> 00:05:01,679
The second said: the missing thing is a receipt. Every answer the Oracle gives

16.1
00:05:01,679 --> 00:05:06,128
arrives without a note saying what it could see when it gave it. Without that note,

16.2
00:05:06,128 --> 00:05:10,578
a grounded answer and a fluent invention are the same string of words.

17.0
00:05:10,578 --> 00:05:14,245
That is the claim the rest of this depends on. From the words alone, a listener with

17.1
00:05:14,245 --> 00:05:17,911
a budget cannot tell the two worlds apart. We wrote it down formally, and we checked

17.2
00:05:17,911 --> 00:05:21,578
it by machine.

18.0
00:05:33,612 --> 00:05:39,779
But the Oracle does more than speak. It computes. And most of what it computes it

18.1
00:05:39,779 --> 00:05:45,945
throws away before the words come out. The words are testimony. The Oracle chooses

18.2
00:05:45,945 --> 00:05:52,112
them. Behind every word is a number it did not choose: how spread out its own next

18.3
00:05:52,112 --> 00:05:58,279
guess was. Call it the pulse. Ask it the shape of wombat scat.

19.0
00:05:58,279 --> 00:06:04,729
It says sausage-shaped. Wombat scat is cube-shaped. And the pulse races on exactly

19.1
00:06:04,729 --> 00:06:11,179
those syllables. Cylindrical. Sausage. Through the soil. Easily eaten.

20.0
00:06:11,179 --> 00:06:15,697
The invention has a pulse, and the pulse is measurable, and it costs almost nothing

20.1
00:06:15,697 --> 00:06:20,215
to export, because the Oracle already computed it.

21.0
00:06:20,215 --> 00:06:25,426
Two things it is not. It is not a lie detector: a calm pulse does not mean true, and

21.1
00:06:25,426 --> 00:06:30,638
the smoothest inventions are calm. And it does not tell you what the Oracle knows.

21.2
00:06:30,638 --> 00:06:35,849
It ranks answers for checking. That's all. That turns out to be enough.

22.0
00:06:48,329 --> 00:06:53,474
Here is the systems question. The kingdom can afford to verify one answer in ten.

22.1
00:06:53,474 --> 00:06:58,620
Which ten? Pick by the pulse and you catch more inventions than picking at random,

22.2
00:06:58,620 --> 00:07:03,765
at every budget we tried, across four families of Oracle.

23.0
00:07:03,765 --> 00:07:13,765
But I promised you receipts, so here is the complication.

24.0
00:07:13,765 --> 00:07:17,790
The words are not useless. An invented answer runs long. Count the words and you

24.1
00:07:17,790 --> 00:07:21,815
catch nearly as many, and on two of the four families, more. The pulse and the word

24.2
00:07:21,815 --> 00:07:25,840
count catch different inventions. That is what the pulse buys: not a better

24.3
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:29,865
detector, a different list.

25.0
00:07:51,833 --> 00:07:56,624
Now the part of the tale I would rather leave out, which demonstrates the problem

25.1
00:07:56,624 --> 00:08:01,414
and why I included it. The tinker had also asked every Oracle how sure it was, on a

25.2
00:08:01,414 --> 00:08:06,205
scale of zero to one hundred, and he found that the sure ones were the wrong ones.

25.3
00:08:06,205 --> 00:08:10,995
He said so, in print, for months. A stranger read the code. The parser took the

25.4
00:08:10,995 --> 00:08:15,786
first number it found in the reply, and the first number in the reply was the zero

25.5
00:08:15,786 --> 00:08:20,576
in scale of zero to one hundred. The finding was retracted. The real result is more

25.6
00:08:20,576 --> 00:08:25,367
useful: the Oracle says one hundred almost everywhere, knowable or not.

26.0
00:08:25,367 --> 00:08:29,678
The tinker's instrument had a painted window too. He did not find it. Someone

26.1
00:08:29,678 --> 00:08:33,990
outside did, by reading what he had been reading for months. That is the reason to

26.2
00:08:33,990 --> 00:08:38,301
export the telemetry rather than trust the testimony, including his.

27.0
00:08:38,301 --> 00:08:43,728
What the tinker kept was not the answers. It was the record: which answers were

27.1
00:08:43,728 --> 00:08:49,155
checked, which were wrong, which instrument was wrong about them, and who found it.

27.2
00:08:49,155 --> 00:08:54,581
The Oracle's problem was never that it invents. Everything that speaks fluently

27.3
00:08:54,581 --> 00:09:00,008
invents. The problem is a room that only hears words. The paper is the receipt.

27.4
00:09:00,008 --> 00:09:05,435
Please retain it.

